{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century. \n*Early Life* \nRaymond III was born around 1140 to Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem his mother was a sister of Queen Melisende of Jerusalem and a daughter of King Baldwin II of Jerusalem. \n*Succession and Marriage* \nRaymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father was killed during an attack on the castle of Tortosa he was around 12 years old at the time. He married Hodierna of Jerusalem's niece also named Hodierna but the marriage was childless. \n*Relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem* \nRaymond III had a complex relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem he was a vassal of the king but he also sought to maintain his independence as Count of Tripoli. He played a significant role in the politics of the kingdom and was involved in the struggle for power between the different factions of the royal court. \n*Military Campaigns* \nRaymond III was involved in several military campaigns during his reign including the Battle of Inab in 1149 and the Battle of Montgisard in 1177. He also led a campaign against the city of Damascus in 1164 but was unsuccessful. \n*Later Life and Death* \nRaymond III died in 1187 without an heir his death led to a succession crisis in the County of Tripoli which was eventually resolved with the appointment of Bohemond IV of Antioch as the new count. \n*Legacy* \nRaymond III is remembered as a significant figure in the history of the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem he played a major role in the politics of the region and was involved in several important military campaigns during his reign.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ra